Code written, then run, on ARM (docs/w4.md W4d). JavaScriptCore's ARM64
assembler names, for each system, how the instruction cache is made to see
code just written. On Kosmos a process does it itself: Nebula sets
SCTLR_EL1.UCT and .UCI (arch/aarch64/mmu.c), so EL0 may read the line
sizes and clean and invalidate by address, which is what the compiler's
__builtin___clear_cache does. The JIT is off for now, so nothing calls it
yet; the header is compiled either way.

--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/assembler/ARM64Assembler.h	2026-11-12 00:34:01.174473040 -0300
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/assembler/ARM64Assembler.h	2026-11-12 00:34:01.192164672 -0300
@@ -4043,6 +4043,8 @@
         linuxPageFlush(current, end);
 #elif OS(WINDOWS)
         FlushInstructionCache(GetCurrentProcess(), code, size);
+#elif OS(KOSMOS)
+        __builtin___clear_cache(static_cast<char*>(code), static_cast<char*>(code) + size);
 #else
 #error "The cacheFlush support is missing on this platform."
 #endif
